            {
                "role": "assistant",
                "username": "RapidGPT",
                "content": "Looking at your IP receiver module (`ip_rx.sv`) and the attached MAC receiver (`mac_rx.sv`), I can help you complete the FSM logic for the header portion. Let me analyze the current implementation and provide improvements.\n\nFor your `ip_rx.sv` HEADER state, here's an improved implementation:\n\n```systemverilog\nHEADER: begin\n    if (mipif.valid_mac) begin\n        next_count = count + 1; \n        case (count) \n            0: begin\n                // First 8 bytes: Process IP version and header length\n                ip_version = mipif.data_mac[63:60];\n                ip_header_length = {4'b0000, mipif.data_mac[59:56]} * 4; // Convert to bytes (×4)\n                \n                // Type of service/DSCP field\n                ip_type = mipif.data_mac[55:48];\n                \n                // Total length\n                ip_total_length = mipif.data_mac[47:32];\n                \n                // Verify it's IPv4\n                if (ip_version != 4'b0100) begin\n                    next_state = ERROR;\n                end\n            end\n            \n            1: begin\n                // Next 8 bytes: Process identification, flags, fragment offset, TTL, protocol\n                ip_identification = mipif.data_mac[63:48];\n                ip_flags = mipif.data_mac[47:45];\n                ip_fragment_offset = mipif.data_mac[44:32];\n                \n                ip_ttl = mipif.data_mac[31:24];\n                ip_protocol = mipif.data_mac[23:16];\n                ip_header_checksum = mipif.data_mac[15:0];\n            end\n            \n            2: begin\n                // Next 8 bytes: Process source and part of destination IP\n                ip_source_address = mipif.data_mac[63:32];\n                ip_destination_address[31:16] = mipif.data_mac[31:16];\n                \n                // Store the rest of destination IP in the next cycle\n                if (ip_header_length == 20) begin // Standard IP header with no options\n                    if (ip_protocol == 8'h11) begin // UDP protocol\n                        next_state = PAYLOAD;\n                        next_valid = 1'b1;\n                    end else begin\n                        next_state = ERROR; // Not UDP, go to error\n                    end\n                end\n            end\n            \n            3: begin\n                // If we have IP options, process them here\n                // For a standard header, we're already in PAYLOAD state\n                \n                // Check if we've processed all header bytes\n                if (count * 8 >= ip_header_length) begin\n                    if (ip_protocol == 8'h11) begin // UDP protocol\n                        next_state = PAYLOAD;\n                        next_valid = 1'b1;\n                    end else begin\n                        next_state = ERROR; // Not UDP, go to error\n                    end\n                end\n            end\n            \n            default: begin\n                // Handle IP headers with options (longer than standard 20 bytes)\n                if (count * 8 >= ip_header_length) begin\n                    if (ip_protocol == 8'h11) begin // UDP protocol\n                        next_state = PAYLOAD;\n                        next_valid = 1'b1;\n                    end else begin\n                        next_state = ERROR; // Not UDP, go to error\n                    end\n                end\n            end\n        endcase\n    end else begin\n        next_state = ERROR; \n    end \nend\n```\n\nImprovements made:\n\n1. **Proper field extraction**: I've organized the bit extraction to match the standard IPv4 header layout, considering your 64-bit data path.\n\n2. **Header length calculation**: Converted the header length field to bytes by multiplying by 4 (as per IPv4 spec).\n\n3. **Protocol verification**: Added checks to ensure you're only processing UDP packets (protocol 0x11).\n\n4. **Variable header length**: Added logic to handle IP headers with options (longer than the standard 20 bytes).\n\n5. **State transition logic**: Made the transitions to PAYLOAD or ERROR states more explicit based on protocol and header processing completion.\n\n6. **Signal timing**: Set next_valid appropriately when transitioning to the PAYLOAD state.\n\nThis implementation assumes your data is organized with the most significant bits first, and that you're processing 8 bytes (64 bits) per cycle. You may need to adjust bit positions if your data alignment is different.",
